<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eldena Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eldena Abbey was a Cistercian monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> on the Pomeranian coast, founded in 1199 under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaromar-i-of-rugen">Jaromar I of Rügen</a>. The monks cleared land, promoted German settlement and founded the salt-trading settlement that grew into Greifswald. The abbey was dissolved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and its brick Gothic ruins were later painted by Caspar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Friedrich.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eldena-abbey.jpg" alt="Eldena Abbey" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harald909 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Klosterruine_Eldena.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
